-- erwartet: N042 allein
-- 988 -- **a foreign lock primitive stays `N042` alone: `N323` is silent.**
--
-- The control beside `beispiele/gift/983`. Same family -- `lock TOR` next to a
-- function the generator's `{Lock}_gib` collides with -- but a FOREIGN body:
-- `extern fn TOR_gib()` has no block for any checker to read. `N042` refuses
-- the name (one C symbol, two declarations: `void TOR_gib(void);` twice is a
-- legal repetition in C, so `cc -Werror` accepts the emitted unit and nothing
-- else in the chain says a word). `N323`
-- (`namen.rs::sperrprimitiv_vertrag`) stays silent BY DESIGN: a foreign body
-- is trust base, not a checked body -- the emitter never defines the prototype,
-- same as `write_cr3`. The name exemption stays where it was; the contract is
-- framework work beside it, not a second verdict on the name.
--
-- The day `N323` fires here, the rule has learned to judge what it cannot see
-- -- this file goes red exactly then (it asserts `N042`, and the reviewer
-- confirms `N323` absent), which is what a control is for.

module gift::sperrprimitiv_fremd {

pub static mut nutz : u32 = 0;

pub lock TOR protects { nutz } rank 0 held <= 8 ops;

-- **Here.** The writer means their own foreign release function; they get the
-- lock primitive's name. No body stands behind it in this unit, so there is
-- nothing `LockGiltAn` could be held against.
extern fn TOR_gib() effects { pure } costs <= 1 ops;

pub impl fn arbeite()
    effects { writes nutz, locks TOR }
    costs   <= 8 ops
{
    locks TOR {
        nutz = 1;
    }
}

}
